<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Forbidden City</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1406">1406</a>–<a href="/years/1420">1420</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Zijincheng, Gugong, Purple Forbidden City, Forbidden Palace, Imperial Palace of the Ming and Qing Dynasties</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Forbidden City is the imperial palace complex at the centre of the Imperial City in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, built from 1406 to 1420 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, with more than a million workers. It was home to 24 Ming and Qing emperors and the centre of political power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1420 to 1924; its name reflects its access, severely restricted to all but the emperor, his family and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> servants. The Outer Court served ceremony, while the Inner Court housed the emperor, the empress and the consorts by rank. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>'s eviction in 1924 the Palace Museum was founded there in 1925, and the complex has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/forbidden-city.jpg" alt="Forbidden City" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N509FZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hall_of_Supreme_Harmony_(20241127120000).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Yongle Emperor moved the capital to Beijing, where the <b>Forbidden City</b> was completed in 1420, restored the Grand Canal to supply it, and sponsored the vast Yongle Encyclopedia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Wanli Emperor (r. 1572–1620) began with able advisers, among them the Grand Secretary Zhang Juzheng, but grew tired of his quarrelling ministers and stayed behind the walls of the <b>Forbidden City</b>, where officials had to bribe powerful eunuchs to have their messages carried to him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>As Li Zicheng's rebels entered Beijing, the Chongzhen Emperor hanged himself on Coal Hill behind the <b>Forbidden City</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He later moved the capital to Beijing and began building the <b>Forbidden City</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Beijing was officially designated capital of the Ming dynasty in the year the <b>Forbidden City</b>, the new imperial residence built from 1406, was completed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
